  "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B028",
        "laureates": [
          "Paul B. Corkum",
          "Maciej Lewenstein"
        ],
        "citation": "for establishing electron recollision as the physical basis of strong-field and attosecond phenomena"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B039",
    "B001"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. Electron recollision gave strong-field physics a simple causal picture: an electron can leave an atom in a laser field, be driven back, and return with energy that explains high-harmonic emission and related ultrafast phenomena. Corkum supplied the decisive physical mechanism, while the Lewenstein formulation made it quantitatively predictive. This is not a retrospective account of a promising platform; it is a mature explanatory advance tested over decades in many experiments and foundational to attosecond science. The two names represent complementary and relatively bounded contributions.",
    "case_against": "The citation must remain narrowly on recollision and its quantitative strong-field theory, rather than silently award the entire later development of attosecond science. Important experimental and theoretical contributors lie outside these two names, and archival scrutiny remains necessary to confirm that the proposed allocation captures the decisive original lineage.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll presents optical lattice clocks as exceptionally reproducible and mature, with a compact attribution to Katori's magic-wavelength concept and Ye's experimental development.",
        "response": "I agree that B039 is the strongest alternative and that its attribution and independent replication set an excellent standard. But B028 ranks ahead for me because it uncovered an unexpected physical mechanism that made a previously puzzling class of phenomena intelligible and predictive. Lattice clocks are an extraordinary extension of precision measurement; recollision changed the physical understanding of laser-driven atomic dynamics itself."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig favors KPZ because its defining theory and Takeuchi's quantitative experiments make a self-contained, mature theory-to-experiment achievement.",
        "response": "That is a compelling comparative standard, and it reinforces the need to demand direct quantitative contact with experiment. On that standard B028 remains strong: the recollision mechanism and Lewenstein model generated concrete, repeatedly tested predictions across strong-field experiments. I retain some deference on the exact breadth of KPZ universality, whereas I can assess more directly the causal and quantitative role of recollision in atomic and molecular physics."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that circuit QED established a distinct, reproducible quantum-optical regime with a coherent allocation among Blais, Wallraff, and Schoelkopf.",
        "response": "I accept B008 as a mature basic-physics achievement, not merely a quantum-computing technology. Its historical boundary nevertheless remains entangled with the broader superconducting-qubit programme. B028 has a narrower central claim: a physical mechanism and its predictive formulation, with a more direct separation from the subsequent expansion of the field."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether historical documentation confirms that Corkum and Lewenstein alone are the most prize-appropriate representatives of the foundational recollision lineage.",
      "How to word the citation so that it recognizes the experimentally confirmed mechanism without extending credit to all later attosecond methods and applications."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No direct personal or institutional connection to the proposed laureates is apparent from the supplied profile. My long-standing work in attosecond photoionization and collaborations with the wider Lund and ETH attosecond communities make subject-matter proximity relevant to disclose, but no specific collaboration with Corkum or Lewenstein is identified."
  }
}
